sweet-ufs-irq (lowclk 2026-11-14; design: docs/research/154-ufs-completion-interrupt.md "Implementation spec", stages 0 and 1 only): UFS command completion by interrupt instead of polling, plus a read-only logging mode, both behind boot-arg sweetufsirq.
Sources [read]: research 154 with its citations: Linux v6.6 drivers/ufs/core/ufshcd.c:6838-6877 (ufshcd_intr: read IS, W1C exactly the bits read, then service; completion = the UTRLDBR scan, :5465-5466), the gs101 devicetree (arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/google/gs101.dtsi ufs@14700000: GIC SPI 532, level high; INTID = SPI + 32 = 564, the convention patch 0347 proved with INTID 541), Linux include/ufs/ufshci.h (IS bit 0 UTRCS, offsets 0x20/0x24/0x4C/0x58), Fuchsia registers.h (UTRLCNR 0x64).
sweetufsirq absent or 0 = polled exactly as before: no register access, no log line, no thread (an invalid value, including 3 = the error-bit stage that is NOT in this patch, logs a GAP and counts as 0). The boot-arg is parsed with the other ufs boot-args in sweet_ufs_init.
sweetufsirq=2 (stage 0, read only): once, in a thread started after su.ready, under the driver lock with UTRLDBR == 0: READ UTRLDBR, UTRIACR (0x4C), UTRLCNR (0x64), IE, IS, VER and print them. In ufs_scsi, after the doorbell cleared and before the driver's own IS write: READ IS, count commands that finish with UTRCS set (is0), with any other bit set (isx), and when UTRCS is set read the OCS byte once and count how often it is still the 0x0f preset (ocs0f; research 154 U3). One row per 5 s: `SWEET UFS IRQ: ro cmds N is0 N (N/5s) isx N ocs0f N`. The polled path itself is unchanged.
sweetufsirq=1 (stage 1, gs101 only): a kernel thread started after su.ready (so the root mount stays polled) takes the driver lock and arms, in this order: UTRLDBR must read 0 (else GAP, not armed); GIC attach of INTID 564 with the SPI DISABLED (patch 0346 sweet_spi_attach_off; rc != 0 = GAP, not armed); clear stale IS (W1C of the bits read); READ UTRIACR and log it (bit 31 = GAP, never written); the ONE NEW WRITE: IE := 0x1 (UTRCS only; the error bits are stage 2), read back (mismatch = disarm + GAP); sweet_spi_enable (failure = disarm + GAP); armed only then.
ISR (interrupt context, no lock, no print): Linux order: read IS and IE, W1C exactly the bits read, read IS again (Linux :6862, read only, a still-set bit is only counted as isr_again), then if IS & IE has UTRCS: bump the sequence counter and thread_wakeup the waiter, return 0 (the line is dropped by the W1C, no DEFER); otherwise count a spurious entry. IS bits other than UTRCS are cleared with the rest and reported as a GAP by the next waiter (never silently discarded). Storm guard: more than 100 spurious entries (IS has no UTRCS) in one second: IE := 0, armed off, return 1 (the dispatcher masks INTID 564), the summary thread finishes the teardown with a loud GAP.
Wait (ufs_scsi, only while armed and in thread context; every other caller keeps the old loop): seq0 is taken before the doorbell write; the doorbell write, the 5 s deadline and the timeout branch (UTRLCLR, ETIMEDOUT) are the old statements; the wait spins CMD_SPIN_NS reading UTRLDBR, then loops: assert_wait with a timeout of min(1 ms, time to the deadline), RE-READ UTRLDBR (clear = done, clear_wait, no block), else thread_block. poll_found counts a completion seen on a timeout wake with no interrupt since the command was issued. 200 consecutive commands with no interrupt at all disarm back to polling with a GAP. After the doorbell clears, while armed the blanket IS write is replaced by: read IS, NO write (the ISR is the only IS writer while armed, as in Linux; a waiter write raced the ISR and made real interrupts count as spurious, review 0354 finding 1), any other bit logged as a GAP; when not armed the driver's own IS write is unchanged. The UTRD write-back wait is unchanged.
Summary row every 5 s while armed: `SWEET UFS IRQ: irqs N spurious N poll_found N cmds N mode irq|off:<why> ...`. Restart: sweet_clean_stop (patch 0083) calls sweet_ufs_irq_quiesce() after the filesystem sync: IE := 0, IS cleared, INTID detached (research 154 risk 7); every OTHER reset path (reboot(2), panic, sweetrestart timer, watchdog handler) goes through sweet_machine_reset_x, whose second statement (after the pets stop) calls sweet_ufs_irq_ie_off(): lock-free IE := 0 through the same judge row, only when IE holds our 0x1, no lock, no print, no detach (review finding 2). Quiesce without the driver lock after 100 ms logs one GAP line.
Hardware access of this patch [the only NEW writes]: IE (0x24) := 0x1 once at arm time and := 0 at every disable (the value the driver already writes at take-over), IS (0x20) W1C of bits that were read set (ISR, arm, disarm; the waiter no longer writes IS while armed), IE := 0 once more at machine reset (lock-free), the GIC calls of patch 0346 for INTID 564 (attach_off, enable, detach). Reads: VER, IS, IE, UTRIACR, UTRLCNR, UTRLDBR. NO write to UTRIACR, UTRLCNR, UTMRLDBR, the vendor block or the UTRLDBR/UTRLCLR doorbell path beyond the existing statements.
Judge: every register access of the new code goes through sui_rd/sui_wr of bsd/dev/sweet_ufs_irq.h: one allowlist (sui_rows[], offset/access/mode) plus value rules (IS: never 0 or 0xFFFFFFFF, only bits read set; IE: 0, or 0x1 only after the attach, before the enable, while IE is 0); a refused access never reaches the bus and disarms. Teardown steps are all tried and idempotent.
Files: new bsd/dev/sweet_ufs_irq.h (pure core between SUI_CORE_BEGIN/END, host test tools/test_ufs_irq_core.py with a fake controller), bsd/dev/sweet_ufs.c (boot-arg, kernel ops, thread, the wait hook in ufs_scsi, sweet_ufs_irq_quiesce), bsd/dev/sweet_restart.c (one call). ufs_write_blocks and its bounds check are not touched. Needs patch 0346 (sweet_spi_attach_off / enable / detach) and every earlier patch that touches sweet_ufs.c (0028 ... 0341); diffed against the fully applied tree. ORDER: after 0351 (0346 for the GIC helpers, 0341 last toucher of sweet_ufs.c).
NOT RUN ON HARDWARE (the kernel glue is built into the lowclk kernels but not host-run; no register of this patch has ever been touched on hardware). The new write path is UNREVIEWED: it needs the rule-8 Opus review (AGENTS.md rule 8) before it boots. The review fixes themselves (lock-free IE := 0 at machine reset, the ISR's IS re-read) are NOT re-reviewed. Review 0354 (docs/reviews/0354-ufs-irq-review.md) findings 1 to 4 are fixed here (ISR sole IS writer while armed, IE := 0 at machine reset, GAP texts and rate limits, IS re-read after the ISR's W1C); findings 5 (pe_fiq.c slot search, patch 0346) and 6 (return 1 counts in the global unhandled counter) are open. The ISR returns 0 (no DEFER) as research 154 s.5.2 suggests and returns 1 only on the storm / ISR-error path.
